WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.120
Part 3. You will hear two students called Bella and Don discussing a presentation they

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:19.080
plan to do on recycling footwear. First you have some time to look at questions 21-24.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Hi Don. Did you get the copy of the article on recycling footwear that I emailed you?

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.480
Yeah, it's here. I've had a look at it.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.439
So do you think it's a good topic for our presentation?

00:01:01.439 --> 00:01:07.680
Well before I started reading it, I thought recycling footwear. Well, although it's quite

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.639
interesting, perhaps there isn't enough to say about it. Because we put shoes in recycling

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:18.120
bins, they go to charity shops and that's about it.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.440
But there's much more to it than that.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:24.760
I realise that now and I'm keen to research the topic more.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:25.959
That's great!

00:01:25.959 --> 00:01:30.639
One of the things I didn't realise until I read the article was just how many pairs

00:01:30.639 --> 00:01:32.519
of trainers get recycled.

00:01:32.519 --> 00:01:38.239
Well, a lot of young people wear them all the time now. They've become more popular

00:01:38.239 --> 00:01:39.879
than ordinary shoes.

00:01:39.879 --> 00:01:45.959
I know. I guess they are very hard-wearing. But don't they look a bit casual for school

00:01:45.959 --> 00:01:49.199
uniform? I don't think they're right for that.

00:01:49.199 --> 00:01:54.440
Actually, I think some of them look quite smart on pupils, better than a scruffy old

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.760
pair of shoes. So do you keep shoes a long time?

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:06.599
Yes, though I do tend to wear my old pairs for doing dirty jobs like cleaning my bike.

00:02:06.599 --> 00:02:12.399
I must admit, I've recycled some perfectly good shoes that haven't gone out of fashion

00:02:12.399 --> 00:02:18.759
and still fit, just because they don't look great on me anymore. That's awful, isn't it?

00:02:18.759 --> 00:02:25.039
I think it's common because there's so much choice. The article did say that recent sales

00:02:25.039 --> 00:02:27.919
of footwear have increased enormously.

00:02:27.919 --> 00:02:29.440
That didn't surprise me.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:36.479
No. But then it said that the amount of recycled footwear has fallen. It's 6% now, compared

00:02:36.479 --> 00:02:42.479
to a previous level of 11%. That doesn't seem to make sense.

00:02:42.479 --> 00:02:48.119
That's because not everything goes through the recycling process. Some footwear just

00:02:48.119 --> 00:02:55.119
isn't good enough to resell, for one reason or another, and gets rejected.

00:03:18.119 --> 00:03:47.600
So, let's find some examples in the article of footwear that was rejected for recycling.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:55.800
OK. I think there are some in the interview with the recycling manager. Yeah, here it is.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:03.320
Hmm. Let's start with the lady's high-heeled shoes. What did he say about those?

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:11.600
He said they were probably expensive. The material was suede and they were beige in colour.

00:04:11.639 --> 00:04:18.440
It looked like someone had only worn them once, but in a very wet field, so the heels

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.720
were too stained with mud and grass to resell them.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:27.399
OK. And the leather ankle boots. What was wrong with them?

00:04:27.399 --> 00:04:34.200
Apparently the heels were worn, but that wasn't the problem. One of the shoes was a much lighter

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:40.320
shade than the other one. It had obviously been left in the sun. I suppose even second-hand

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.640
shoes should look the same. Sure. Then there were the red baby shoes.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:53.480
Oh yes. We're told to tie shoes together when we put them in a recycling bin, but people

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:57.200
often don't bother. You'd think it would have been easy to find

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:04.480
the other, but it wasn't. That was a shame because they were obviously new.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:09.839
The trainers were interesting. He said they looked like they'd been worn by a marathon

00:05:09.839 --> 00:05:13.679
runner. Yeah. Weren't they split?

00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:19.239
Not exactly. One of the soles was so worn under the foot that you could put your finger

00:05:19.239 --> 00:05:23.480
through it. Well, we could certainly use some of those

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:29.600
examples in our presentation to explain why 90% of shoes that people take to recycling

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:36.799
centres or bins get thrown into landfill. What did you think about the project his team

00:05:36.799 --> 00:05:43.040
set up to avoid this by making new shoes out of the good parts of old shoes?

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:50.359
It sounded like a good idea. They get so many shoes, they should be able to match parts.

00:05:50.359 --> 00:05:56.559
I wasn't surprised that it failed though. I mean, who wants to buy second-hand shoes,

00:05:56.559 --> 00:06:02.320
really? Think of all the germs you could catch! Well, people didn't refuse them for that

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:07.399
reason, did they? It was because the pairs of shoes weren't identical.

00:06:07.399 --> 00:06:13.200
They still managed to ship them overseas though. That's another area we need to discuss.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:18.279
You know, I used to consider this topic just from my own perspective, by thinking about

00:06:18.279 --> 00:06:24.679
my own recycling behaviour without looking at the bigger picture. So much happens once

00:06:24.679 --> 00:06:30.279
shoes leave the recycling area. It's not as simple as you first think, and

00:06:30.279 --> 00:06:34.480
we can show that by taking a very different approach to it.

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:42.480
Absolutely. So, let's discuss how we're going to split up the…

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:49.119
That is the end of Part 3. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.

